        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To find the <strong>cube root</strong> of a number <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>, we must ask ourselves, &quot;what number <em>cubed</em> equals <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>?&quot;</p>",
            "readable_text": "To find the cube root of a number [MATH: x], we must ask ourselves, \"what number cubed equals [MATH: x]?\"",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, the cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 8]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2]</span> since \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,]</span>\nand the cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -8]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -2]</span> since\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, the cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2] since\n[MATH: 2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,]\nand the cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2] since\n[MATH: (-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In general, the cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x^3]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "In general, the cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Like the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3]</span> next to it, i.e., <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.]</span> Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Like the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little [MATH: 3] next to it, i.e., [MATH: \\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.] Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<ul><li><p>&quot;The cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 8]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2]</span>&quot; can be expressed as <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{8}=2.]</span></p></li><li><p>&quot;The cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -8]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -2]</span>&quot; can be expressed as  <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.]</span></p></li><li><p>&quot;The cube root of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x^3]</span> is <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>&quot; can be expressed as  <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.]</span></p></li></ul>",
            "readable_text": "- \"The cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{8}=2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> For square roots, we have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,]</span> but for cube roots, we have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.]</span> No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because</p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: For square roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,] but for cube roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.] No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<ul><li><p>the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and</p></li><li><p>the cube root of a negative number is a negative number.</p></li></ul>",
            "readable_text": "- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo find the cube root of a number [MATH: x], we must ask ourselves, \"what number cubed equals [MATH: x]?\"\n\nContent:\nFor example, the cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2] since\n[MATH: 2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,]\nand the cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2] since\n[MATH: (-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.]\n\nContent:\nIn general, the cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x.]\n\nContent:\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little [MATH: 3] next to it, i.e., [MATH: \\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.] Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\nContent:\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{8}=2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.]\n\nContent:\nNote: For square roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,] but for cube roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.] No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\nContent:\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number."
      },
