    ],
    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo find the cube root of a number [MATH: x], we must ask ourselves, \"what number cubed equals [MATH: x]?\"\n\nContent:\nFor example, the cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2] since\n[MATH: 2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,]\nand the cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2] since\n[MATH: (-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.]\n\nContent:\nIn general, the cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x.]\n\nContent:\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little [MATH: 3] next to it, i.e., [MATH: \\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.] Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\nContent:\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: 8] is [MATH: 2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{8}=2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: -8] is [MATH: -2]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.]\n- \"The cube root of [MATH: x^3] is [MATH: x]\" can be expressed as [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.]\n\nContent:\nNote: For square roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,] but for cube roots, we have [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.] No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\nContent:\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Finding the Cube Root of a Monomial\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{-8x^6}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n[MATH: root(\u2212 8x^{6}, 3)|=; root((\u2212 2x^{2}) \u22c5 (\u2212 2x^{2}) \u22c5 (\u2212 2x^{2}), 3)|=; root((\u2212 2x^{2})^{3}, 3)|=; \u2212 2x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: root(125x^{3}y^{3}, 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5xy]\nb. [MATH: 5x^{2}y]\nc. [MATH: 5xy^{2}]\nd. [MATH: 5x^{2}y^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: root(27x^{3}, 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3x^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 9x^{2}]\nc. [MATH: 9x]\nd. [MATH: 3x]\ne. [MATH: 3x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Finding Cube Roots of Terms Containing Binomials\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{(5x - 4)^3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n[MATH: root((5x \u2212 4)^{3}, 3)|= 5x \u2212 4.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: root(27(x \u2212 1)^{3}, 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 27(x \u2212 1)]\nb. [MATH: 9(x \u2212 1)]\nc. [MATH: 3(1 \u2212 x)]\nd. [MATH: 3(x \u2212 1)]\ne. [MATH: 3(x \u2212 1)^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: root((2x \u2212 1)^{3}, 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2root(x, 3) \u2212 1]\nb. [MATH: 2x]\nc. [MATH: 8x]\nd. [MATH: root(8x \u2212 1, 3)]\ne. [MATH: 2x \u2212 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Finding the Cube Root of a Fraction\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n[MATH: root((a^{3})/((b \u2212 1)^{3}), 3)|=; root((a)/((b \u2212 1)) \u22c5 (a)/((b \u2212 1)) \u22c5 (a)/((b \u2212 1)), 3)|=; root(((a)/(b \u2212 1))^{3}, 3)|=; (a)/(b \u2212 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: root(\u2212 (64)/(b^{3}), 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (4)/(b^{2})]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(b)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (4)/(b)]\nd. [MATH: (4)/(b)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (8)/(b)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: root((8)/(x^{3}), 3).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (4)/(x^{2})]\nb. [MATH: (4)/(x)]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(x^{3})]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(x)]\ne. [MATH: (8)/(x)]"
